Every source file also names its license on its first line (after any shebang)
with an `SPDX-License-Identifier` comment, which the build keeps in `dist/`.


MIT  (full text: LICENSE-MIT)
-----------------------------

The port of calendarium-romanum, which its author licenses under "GNU LGPL 3 or
MIT, at the licensee's choice". This package takes the MIT option.

    src/core/                     -> dist/core/
    src/data/sanctorale-files.ts  -> dist/data/sanctorale-files.js
    src/data/locales.ts           -> dist/data/locales.js
    test/core/

`roman-catholic-church-calendar/core` imports nothing outside these paths, so
that entry point is entirely MIT. test/licensing.test.ts enforces this.


MIT OR LGPL-3.0-or-later  (licensee's choice; texts: LICENSE-MIT, COPYING.LESSER)
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Code Source & Summit wrote for this package that is not derived from either Ruby
project: the HTTP adapters, and the entry points, barrels and tooling around
them. You may use it under either license.

    src/http/adapters/
    src/http/types.ts
    src/http/index.ts
    src/index.ts
    src/data/index.ts
    bin/
    lambda/
    scripts/

Copyright (c) 2026 Source & Summit

The choice covers these files only. Most of them import LGPL code (the adapters
call src/http/router.ts, for example), and that code stays LGPL, so the
`roman-catholic-church-calendar` and `/http` entry points as a whole are still
LGPL.


